--TEST--
The five ways an `array<T>` is asked for something it does not have: a second element type in its literal, a string form for `echo`, a `+` over two of them, a key binding that is not a `string`, and a `float` subscript (`rule:types/arrays`, `rule:types/array-combination`, `rule:types/conversion`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/array-t

// `rule:types/arrays`: an array literal is checked against the element type
// where it is written, so one entry of another type refuses there.
array<int> $mixed = [1, "two"];

// `rule:types/conversion`: an array has no text of its own, so `echo`, which
// converts what it is given to `string`, has nothing to convert it to.
array<int> $a = [1, 2];
echo $a, "\n";

// `rule:types/array-combination`: binary `+` over two arrays is a diagnostic
// naming the one operation it ever meant.
array<int> $b = [3];
array<int> $joined = $a + $b;

// `rule:types/arrays`: every key is stored as a `string`, so no array could
// ever fill a key binding declared as anything else.
foreach ($a as int $k => int $v) {
    echo $k, $v;
}

// `rule:types/arrays`: a `float`, `bool` or `null` subscript is refused where
// PHP truncates or stringifies it.
float $f = 1.0;
$a[$f] = 2;
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `int`, found `string`
%A
error[E0707]: `array<int>` has no string form
%A
error[E0467]: `+` is not defined with an array operand
%A
error[E0723]: an array's key binding is a `string`, not `int`
%A
error[E0434]: a `float`, `bool`, or `null` array key is not allowed
%A
error: aborting due to 5 errors
